# notes of slides 1-34 of the chapter 5 deck 3.8.0 (reader-facing, 60-140 words, no presenter direction, no everyday analogy)
N = {}
N[1] = """This chapter explains how a Bitcoin wallet is recovered after a loss. A wallet does not hold coins; it holds keys, and the keys can be lost, so the chapter follows one seed from twelve recovery words to a whole tree of keys and to a backup that has been tested. The deck has 68 slides in seven parts, 27 code examples with their printed results and three true stories with real names and dates. The figure comes from Mastering Bitcoin, third edition, and every code example runs on the course page under Run slide code with the same library and the same data."""
N[2] = """The route has seven parts that follow the order of the chapter: keys in wallets, recovery codes, the BIP39 stack, the HD wallet, paths and descriptors, data that is not keys, and deploying and testing. Each row names the slides of its part, so a reader can jump straight to a part. The three counters give the size of the deck: 68 slides, 27 code examples with their results and three true stories. The last row covers the summary, the resources and the questions. A reader who finishes the deck should hold a backup that has been tested by a restore."""
N[3] = """The seven questions are the questions of the chapter, written so that each one has its answer in a part of the deck. They begin with what a wallet contains and end with what must be backed up and how the backup is tested. In between they ask how a recovery code becomes a seed, how one seed makes a tree of keys, which recovery schemes exist, which derivation paths wallets use and where the book differs from the standards. The summary near the end returns to the same questions. A reader who can answer all seven without looking has understood the chapter."""
N[4] = """The chapter has eight branches, and the bars count the key concepts that the course page explains in each one. The hierarchical deterministic wallet has the most with 25, followed by derivation paths with 15, then the BIP39 stack and recovery codes with 14 each. Wallet keys has 10, backing up data that is not keys has 9, and the foundations and the deployment branch have 8 each. The seven parts of the deck follow these branches, every part opens with a divider, and each code slide shows the code, a note beside it and the printed result."""
N[5] = """In 2011 Stefan Thomas, a programmer who had been technology chief at Ripple, was paid 7,002 bitcoin and kept the keys on an IronKey USB drive in a safe. He lost the paper with the password. On 12 January 2021 the reporter Nathaniel Popper told the story in the New York Times: eight of the ten allowed guesses were already used, and the drive locks for good after the tenth. Memory was his only backup. The lesson is that a recovery code tested in advance is the alternative to a password held in the head. The photograph shows Thomas in 2015."""
N[6] = """Every code slide of this deck calls the same small Python library, which was written for reading and uses only the standard library. Its functions turn entropy into recovery words, words into a 512-bit seed, a seed into a master key, a parent into a child, a path into a key, a public key into a bc1q address, and a text into a descriptor checksum. Shamir splitting and the gap-limit scan are also included. The sample data table names the values that return on slide after slide: the book's 128 bits and twelve words, the BIP32 test seed, the BIP84 test code, a descriptor made by Bitcoin Core and the book's extended public key."""
N[7] = """Part 1 asks what a wallet really holds. Six concepts are defined here: the wallet database, which is the file where a wallet keeps keys and notes; independent key generation, in which every key is made alone and needs its own backup; deterministic key generation, in which every key follows from one seed by a rule; the seed itself; the key tweak, a number added to a key to make a related key; and the hardware signing device, which signs without showing its keys. The diagram shows the central idea: one seed and one rule give key 0, key 1 and key 2, so a single backup covers them all."""
N[8] = """A wallet is more than one program. The wallet application prepares a payment, the wallet database supplies the keys, a signing device signs, and the network receives the signed transaction. The four cards define the wallet database, a wallet that holds public keys only, external signing and the hardware signing device. A wallet with public keys only can see the funds sent to its keys and show them, but it cannot spend. External signing splits the work between one program that prepares the transaction and another program or device that signs it. Keys are the wallet; everything else can be rebuilt from them."""
N[9] = """The book shows three ways to make keys. In the first, loose keys are made independently and each one needs its own backup. In the second, deterministic generation derives keys k0 to kn from one seed, so one backup is enough. In the third, hierarchical generation grows a tree of keys below the seed, which lets a wallet organise keys by purpose and account. The two figures come from Mastering Bitcoin, third edition. The difference matters for recovery: independent keys multiply the number of secrets to protect, while seed-based keys reduce the secrets to a single one."""
N[10] = """This table lists the concepts of part 1 together with the slides on which the deck shows each one and a short statement of what it means in the wallet. The wallet database is the file that stores keys and notes; independent key generation makes each key alone, so each needs its own backup; deterministic key generation means one seed and a rule always give the same keys. The seed is the one secret every key comes from, a key tweak is a number added to a key to make a related key, and a hardware signing device signs and never reveals its keys. The last two rows are the chapter itself and its technology stack."""
N[11] = """The code defines a seed and a rule that turns it into key number i by hashing the seed together with the index with SHA-256. It then asks the rule for keys 0, 1 and 2 and prints the first eight characters of each: aa74b609, 94af53de and fc092c45. Running the same lines again gives exactly the same keys, and the comparison prints True. This is the whole argument for deterministic wallets: one seed to back up, and every key follows from it. The example is code 1 of 27, and it runs on the course page under Run slide code with the chapter library."""
N[12] = """Part 2 asks how a seed is written down so that a human can keep it. Six concepts are defined: a recovery code, which is words or characters that stand for the seed; BIP39, which uses words from a list of 2,048 and a checksum; the rival schemes Electrum v2, Aezeed and Muun, which add versions, dates or encryption; SLIP39 and codex32, which split one secret into shares that can be recombined; the recovery code passphrase, an extra secret that changes the wallet; and the brainwallet, words chosen by the owner, which is not a recovery code. The diagram runs from entropy to a written code that is restored later."""
N[13] = """Six recovery code schemes are in use, and each adds something to the same goal. BIP39 uses words from a list of 2,048, a checksum and an optional passphrase. Electrum version 2 has no global word list and stores a version number inside the code. Aezeed, from the lnd wallet, adds two version numbers and a wallet birthday. Muun uses a non-word code that decrypts the keys in a separate document. SLIP39 splits one seed into several codes, for example five made and three needed. Codex32 lets shares be made and checked by hand with paper and a pen. The schemes differ in reliability, privacy and the ability to split."""
N[14] = """Words make a recovery code easy to remember, yet the book strongly urges writing it down, because memory fails. A brainwallet is made of words that the owner chose, whereas a recovery code is random and made by the wallet; chosen words are easy to guess. Coercion is a physical risk: someone who thinks you remember a code may force you to reveal it. The book cites over 100 attacks, and the 2026 copy of the list has 364 rows. A wallet birthday is a date stored in the code so that a restore need not scan the whole blockchain. The rule is to write it down, make it random and not depend on memory."""
N[15] = """This table lists the recovery schemes of part 2 with the slides that show them and what each one is. The recovery code is a set of words that stands for the seed, and BIP39 is the most common scheme. Electrum v2 has no global list and puts a version inside the code. Aezeed has versions and a wallet birthday, and Muun is a code that decrypts a separate document. SLIP39 gives shares of one seed with a threshold, and Codex32 gives shares and checksums made with paper and a pen. All seven answer the same question, how to write a seed down, with different trade-offs between reliability, privacy and splitting."""
N[16] = """This table covers the choices that surround a recovery code. A passphrase is an extra secret mixed into the seed, so the same words with a different passphrase give a different wallet. Plausible deniability means a wallet that looks genuine and holds little, and a duress wallet is a decoy wallet opened by another passphrase and handed over under threat. A brainwallet is made of words the owner chose and is easy to guess. The wallet birthday is the creation date stored in the code. The last two rows are the book sections on seeds and recovery codes and on recovery code passphrases. If the passphrase is lost, the words alone open nothing."""
N[17] = """Two schemes check themselves differently. The first line checks a codex32 string, which carries a long checksum over the whole text that can be verified by hand, and the run prints valid: True. Electrum marks a seed by the first digits of an HMAC, so the search tries seed 0, 1, 2 and so on until the HMAC begins with the digits 100. It stops at seed number 5483. BIP39, in contrast, takes its checksum bits from SHA-256, which a later slide shows. Every scheme has its own safety net, so a wallet can tell a mistyped code from a valid one. This is code 2 of 27."""
N[18] = """The example is a small model of the idea behind SLIP39. A secret is split into five shares such that any three can rebuild it. The run combines shares 1, 2 and 3 and then shares 2, 3 and 4, and both groups give back the secret, printing True twice. Two shares, 1 and 2, give a wrong answer, and the run prints False. Two shares therefore reveal nothing about the secret, and three recover all of it. The model uses a polynomial over a prime field, which is the same principle that real share schemes apply. This is code 3 of 27 on the course page."""
N[19] = """Part 3 follows the BIP39 stack from random bits to a seed. Six concepts are defined: entropy, the random bits the code is made from; the checksum, a few bits that reveal a typo; the word list of 2,048 numbered words that everyone shares; the key-stretching function, repeated work that makes guessing slow; PBKDF2 with its salt, the stretcher BIP39 uses and the string it adds; and the root seed, the 512-bit result that starts the tree. The diagram shows the order: entropy, a checksum, eleven-bit pieces, words, the PBKDF2 stretch and the 512-bit seed."""
N[20] = """Marek Palatinus and Pavol Rusnak of SatoshiLabs, the makers of Trezor, saw that nobody transcribes 128 raw bits correctly, and Aaron Voisine and Sean Bowe joined them. On 10 September 2013 they published BIP 39 in the public bips repository on GitHub, a way to write entropy as ordinary words. The recipe is 2,048 words of eleven bits each, a checksum folded in and key stretching from the words to the seed. The recovery code is therefore an interface standard between humans and entropy. The photograph shows Trezor One devices, and the link goes to the specification."""
N[21] = """A recovery code is generated in five steps. First, make 128 bits of entropy for twelve words. Second, hash it with SHA-256. Third, append the first four bits of the hash as the checksum, which gives 132 bits. Fourth, split the 132 bits into twelve pieces of eleven bits. Fifth, look each piece up in the word list of 2,048 words. The arithmetic is twelve words times eleven bits, which equals 132 bits, which equals 128 bits of entropy plus 4 bits of checksum. Longer codes use more entropy: the book allows 128 to 256 bits, which gives 12 to 24 words."""
N[22] = """SHA-256 is the hash function inside BIP39, and this example shows its main property. Two inputs that differ by one letter, seed A and seed B, are hashed, and the two 256-bit results are combined with XOR so that every differing bit becomes a one. Counting the ones gives 126 of 256 bits, about half. A tiny change in the input therefore makes a huge difference in the output, which is why a checksum taken from a hash detects even a single mistyped word. This is code 4 of 27, and it uses only the hashlib module of the standard library."""
N[23] = """BIP39 allows five sizes of entropy: 128, 160, 192, 224 and 256 bits. The loop turns an all-zero entropy of each size into a recovery code and counts the words. The counts are 12, 15, 18, 21 and 24, so each extra 32 bits adds three words. The result line shows the last pair, 256 bits and 24 words. More bits mean more words and a greater security strength, which is why a wallet that protects a large holding may choose 24 words, while 12 words from 128 bits is the usual default. This is code 5 of 27 on the course page."""
N[24] = """The chapter's 128 bits of entropy, written as the hexadecimal string 0c1e24e5917779d297e14d45f14e1a1a, become its twelve words, and the comparison with the book's words prints True. Each word is a position in the BIP39 list, from 0 to 2047, and the table shows the twelve positions: 96, 1929, 459, 279, 956, 1866, 764, 333, 559, 1107, 1076 and 423. The first word, army, is position 96, which is the eleven bits 00001100000. Every word is therefore just an eleven-bit number. This is code 6 of 27, and the word list is read from the file that the library ships."""
N[25] = """The first lines decode the recovery code back to entropy and print the entropy, 0c1e24e5917779d297e14d45f14e1a1a, together with True, which says that the last four bits match the checksum recomputed from the entropy. The second part changes the last word from crunch to army. The recomputed checksum no longer matches, so the check prints False and the decoded entropy is not trusted. A checksum turns a silent typo into a loud error. It cannot repair the typo, and it cannot catch every change, but it catches most. This is code 7 of 27 on the course page."""
N[26] = """The same word can be written in two ways: the letter e with an acute accent as one character, or an e followed by a combining accent. In the example the word cafe with an accent has 4 characters as typed and 5 after normalisation with the NFKD form. BIP39 requires the text to be normalised before stretching, so both spellings give the same seed, and the comparison prints True. Without this rule a wallet restored on another device could derive a different seed from the same visible words. This is code 8 of 27, and it uses the unicodedata module of the standard library."""
N[27] = """PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA512 stretches the recovery words by repeating the work many times. The example hashes the same words with three settings and prints the first 12 hexadecimal digits: one round with the salt mnemonic gives d4a21305b044, 2048 rounds with the same salt give 5b56c417303f, and 2048 rounds with the salt mnemonic1 give 6b63963292ef. BIP39 uses 2048 rounds. The three results are unrelated, so a change of rounds or salt changes everything. The slowness is deliberate: each guess of a passphrase costs 2048 rounds. This is code 9 of 27 on the course page."""
N[28] = """A passphrase opens a different wallet. The example fixes one key path, m/84h/0h/0h/0/0, and defines a helper that derives the key from the words together with a passphrase and returns its address. With no passphrase the first address is bc1qg0azlj4w2lrq8jssrrz6eprt2fe7f7edm4vpd5; with the passphrase SuperDuperSecret it is bc1qp2dvcxvj4y5p5mesfgspljzdvnyzmqy2724n04. The twelve words are the same in both cases. The passphrase is therefore a second secret that must be backed up separately, and without it the words alone open the wrong wallet. This is code 10 of 27."""
N[29] = """This table lists the concepts of part 3 with the slides that show them. Entropy is the random bits a code is made from, the checksum is bits that reveal a typo, and the word list gives 2,048 words for eleven bits each. The key-stretching function is repeated hashing that makes guessing slow, PBKDF2 is the stretcher BIP39 uses with HMAC-SHA512, and the salt is a string that separates equal inputs. The root seed is the 512-bit seed that starts the tree. Together they describe how a recovery code becomes a seed, from entropy to the final 512 bits."""
N[30] = """The second table of part 3 lists the sections of the book. The section on BIP39 recovery codes is the standard that turns bits into words. Generating a recovery code covers entropy, checksum, eleven-bit pieces and words. From recovery code to seed covers stretching the words into a 512-bit seed. How much entropy you need covers the range of 128 to 256 bits, which is 12 to 24 words. The optional passphrase section explains the passphrase mixed into the salt. Each row names the slides where the deck shows the idea, so a reader can go from the section of the book to the code that runs."""
N[31] = """Part 4 grows a tree from one seed. Six concepts are defined: the master private key, the first private key of the tree; the master chain code, the 32 bytes that let the tree keep growing; child key derivation, a new key from a parent, a chain code and an index; hardened derivation, a step that only the private parent key can take; the extended key, a key and its chain code written as one string; and the index number, which picks the child, 0 to 2 to the power 31 minus 1 for normal children and above that for hardened ones. The diagram runs from the root seed through HMAC-SHA512 to the master key and extended keys."""
N[32] = """The tree has a seed at the top and a master key, called m, below it. The master key has children m/0, m/1 and m/2, and each child has children of its own, such as m/0/0 and m/0/1, so every node is named by its path. Every node is a key and a chain code, 32 bytes each. The master is made by one HMAC of the seed. A child is made from its parent, the parent's chain code and an index. A backup of the seed therefore backs up the whole tree, because every node can be derived again from the root."""
N[33] = """The example computes the master key from the BIP32 test seed. HMAC-SHA512 with the key Bitcoin seed gives 64 bytes, and the example splits them into two halves of 32 bytes. The left half is the master private key and the right half is the master chain code. The library function master does the same in one call, and the comparison prints True True. The printed key begins e8f32e723decf405 and has 64 hexadecimal digits. Half of the HMAC is the key and half is the chain code. This is code 11 of 27, and the diagram shows the seed of 16 bytes splitting into the two halves."""
N[34] = """Child key derivation starts at the master pair and asks for children 0, 1 and 2. Each child receives its own private key and its own chain code, so the tree can keep growing. The table shows the first 12 hexadecimal digits: child 0 has the key 4e2cdcf2f14e and chain code d323f1be5af3, child 1 has 4df92b81d272 and 8dd96414ff4d, and child 2 has 271614f2ca44 and 6da671c6fba4. Three indexes give three unrelated children. The full values are 64 digits each. This is code 12 of 27 on the course page, and the chain code is what keeps the derivation unpredictable."""
